WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:16.507
The archive.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:20.659
One may envision rooms and shelves

00:00:20.683 --> 00:00:23.147
stocked with boxes
and cartons of old stuff.

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:27.298
And yet, for those who are
patient enough to dig through it,

00:00:27.322 --> 00:00:30.099
the archive provides
the precious opportunity

00:00:30.123 --> 00:00:31.877
to touch the past,

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:34.426
to feel and learn from the experiences

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.453
of once-living people who now seem
dead and buried deeply in the archive.

00:00:40.038 --> 00:00:44.363
But what if there was a way
to bring the archive to life?

00:00:44.387 --> 00:00:47.226
Jon Michael Reese: "The world
is thinking wrong about race."

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.453
Melissa Joyner: "This country insists
upon judging the Negro."

00:00:50.477 --> 00:00:52.056
JMR: "Because it does not know."

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.049
AYGTK: What if one could make it breathe?

00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:56.731
MJ: "By his lowest
and most vicious representatives."

00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:57.905
AYGTK: Speak.

00:00:57.929 --> 00:00:59.968
JMR: "An honest, straightforward exhibit."

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:01.311
AYGTK: And even sing to us,

00:01:01.335 --> 00:01:03.752
so that the archive
becomes accessible to everyone.

00:01:03.776 --> 00:01:06.307
What would performing
the archive look like?

00:01:06.331 --> 00:01:09.447
A performance that is not
simply based on a true story

00:01:10.264 --> 00:01:13.598
but one that allows us
to come face-to-face

00:01:13.622 --> 00:01:16.188
with things we thought
were once dead and buried.

00:01:16.212 --> 00:01:17.229
(Piano music)

00:01:17.253 --> 00:01:20.006
This is what "At Buffalo,"
a new musical we're developing,

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.188
is all about.

00:01:21.212 --> 00:01:24.744
Using collections
from over 30 archival institutions,

00:01:24.768 --> 00:01:30.572
"At Buffalo" performs the massive archive
of the 1901 Pan-American Exposition,

00:01:30.596 --> 00:01:32.619
the first World's Fair
of the 20th century,

00:01:32.643 --> 00:01:33.914
held in Buffalo, New York.

00:01:33.938 --> 00:01:35.619
Now, if you've heard of this fair,

00:01:35.643 --> 00:01:38.942
it might be because this is where
then-US president, William McKinley,

00:01:38.966 --> 00:01:40.132
was assassinated.

00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:42.379
For nearly 17 years,

00:01:42.403 --> 00:01:45.474
I've stayed inside the gates
and the archive of this fair,

00:01:45.498 --> 00:01:47.482
not only because of that story

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:50.958
but because of a real
life-and-death racial drama

00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:53.387
that played out on the fairgrounds.

00:01:53.411 --> 00:01:56.696
Here, in a place that was like
Disney World, the Olympics,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.974
carnivals, museums, all in one,

00:01:58.998 --> 00:02:03.236
there were three conflicting displays
of what it meant to be black

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.807
in the United States.

00:02:04.831 --> 00:02:07.544
The archive says white showmen presented

00:02:07.568 --> 00:02:09.687
a savage black origin

00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:12.846
in the form of 98 West
and Central Africans,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.576
living and performing war dances

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.160
in a recreated village
called Darkest Africa.

00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:21.069
And across the street,

00:02:21.093 --> 00:02:23.966
a happy slave life,

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.704
in the form of 150 Southern
black performers,

00:02:27.728 --> 00:02:29.085
picking cotton,

00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:31.355
singing and dancing minstrel shows

00:02:31.379 --> 00:02:36.426
in a recreated antebellum attraction
called Old Plantation.

00:02:37.284 --> 00:02:38.990
As a response,

00:02:39.014 --> 00:02:43.021
the black Buffalo community championed
the third display of blackness:

00:02:43.506 --> 00:02:44.706
the Negro Exhibit.

00:02:45.395 --> 00:02:49.014
Codesigned by African American
scholar W.E.B. Du Bois,

00:02:49.038 --> 00:02:53.673
it curated photographs,
charts, books and more,

00:02:53.697 --> 00:02:56.626
to show black Americans
as a high-achieving race,

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.430
capable of education and progress.

00:03:00.373 --> 00:03:02.624
When I first encountered this story,

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:04.957
I understood from my own life experience

00:03:04.981 --> 00:03:07.997
what was at stake to have members
of the African diaspora

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.688
see each other like this.

00:03:10.046 --> 00:03:14.188
For me, as the child of immigrant parents
from Ghana, West Africa,

00:03:14.212 --> 00:03:16.156
born in the American South,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.925
raised in Manhattan, Kansas,

00:03:18.949 --> 00:03:20.124
(Laughter)

00:03:20.148 --> 00:03:23.275
and having attended the same
elite school as Du Bois,

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:26.037
I could see that the Buffalo fair
effectively pitted

00:03:26.061 --> 00:03:28.126
the black Northerner
against the Southerner,

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.989
the educated against the uneducated,

00:03:30.013 --> 00:03:33.195
and the African American
against the African.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.298
And I wanted to know:

00:03:35.322 --> 00:03:40.369
How did these three distinct groups
of black folk navigate this experience?

00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:44.871
Unfortunately, the archive
had answers to questions like this

00:03:44.895 --> 00:03:47.172
underneath racial caricature,

00:03:47.196 --> 00:03:50.775
conflicting information
and worse -- silence.

00:03:51.212 --> 00:03:54.307
(Piano music)

00:03:54.331 --> 00:03:58.739
Still, I could hear musical melodies

00:03:58.763 --> 00:04:00.046
and see dance numbers

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:01.454
and the rhythms of the words

00:04:01.478 --> 00:04:03.977
coming off the pages
of old newspaper articles.

00:04:04.001 --> 00:04:05.675
And learning that this World's Fair

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:09.641
had music playing everywhere
on its fairgrounds,

00:04:09.665 --> 00:04:13.561
I knew that live, immersive,
spectacular musical theater,

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:16.601
with the latest technologies of our time,

00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:21.260
is the closest experience that can bring
the archival story of the 1901 fair

00:04:21.284 --> 00:04:24.060
out of boxes and into life.

00:04:24.585 --> 00:04:27.600
Stories, like Tannie and Henrietta,

00:04:27.624 --> 00:04:30.409
a husband and wife vaudeville duo in love

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:33.553
who become at odds over performing
these "coon" minstrel shows

00:04:33.577 --> 00:04:35.877
while striving for their
five-dollar-a-week dream

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:37.918
in the Old Plantation attraction.

00:04:37.942 --> 00:04:40.657
Like African businessman John Tevi,

00:04:40.681 --> 00:04:42.363
from present-day Togo,

00:04:42.387 --> 00:04:45.791
who must outwit the savage rules
of the human zoo

00:04:45.815 --> 00:04:47.926
in which he has become trapped.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.490
And stories like Mary Talbert,

00:04:51.514 --> 00:04:53.990
a wealthy leader
of the black Buffalo elite,

00:04:54.014 --> 00:04:55.216
who must come to terms

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.879
with the racial realities
of her home town.

00:04:57.903 --> 00:04:59.928
MJ: "The dominant race in this country

00:04:59.952 --> 00:05:01.990
insists upon judging the Negro

00:05:02.014 --> 00:05:04.545
by his lowest and most
vicious representatives."

00:05:04.569 --> 00:05:06.879
AYGTK: Like Old Plantation
and Darkest Africa.

00:05:06.903 --> 00:05:10.093
MJ: "... instead of by the more
intelligent and worthy classes."

00:05:10.117 --> 00:05:12.558
AYGTK: When fair directors
ignored Mary Talbert

00:05:12.582 --> 00:05:16.117
and the local black Buffalo community's
request to participate in the fair,

00:05:16.141 --> 00:05:18.275
newspapers say that Mary Talbert

00:05:18.299 --> 00:05:20.887
and her club of educated
African American women

00:05:20.911 --> 00:05:23.252
held a rousing protest meeting.

00:05:23.276 --> 00:05:25.093
But the details of that meeting,

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:27.410
even down to the fiery speech she gave,

00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:29.426
were not fully captured in the archive.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.212
So, "At Buffalo" takes the essence
of Mary's speech

00:05:33.236 --> 00:05:35.421
and turns it into song.

00:05:36.251 --> 00:05:39.122
(All singing) We must, we are unanimous.

00:05:39.744 --> 00:05:43.527
We must, we are unanimous.

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:46.003
MJ: We've got something to show --

00:05:46.027 --> 00:05:49.717
we're going to teach a lesson in Buffalo.

00:05:49.741 --> 00:05:53.302
It would benefit the nation

00:05:53.326 --> 00:05:57.365
to see our growth since emancipation.

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:03.445
Colored people should be represented
in this Pan-American exposition,

00:06:03.469 --> 00:06:07.064
it would benefit the nation

00:06:07.088 --> 00:06:11.492
to see our growth since emancipation.

00:06:11.516 --> 00:06:17.988
(All singing) They made a great mistake

00:06:18.351 --> 00:06:23.951
not to appoint someone from the race.

00:06:25.382 --> 00:06:28.103
We must, we are unanimous.

00:06:28.844 --> 00:06:32.325
We must, we are unanimous.

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:33.915
We must, we are unanimous.

00:06:33.939 --> 00:06:38.423
AYGTK: Mary Talbert successfully demands
that the Negro Exhibit come to the fair.

00:06:38.805 --> 00:06:41.162
And to have the Negro Exhibit in Buffalo

00:06:41.186 --> 00:06:45.868
means that the musical must tell the story
behind why Du Bois cocreated it ...

00:06:47.694 --> 00:06:52.234
and why Mary and the black elite
felt it was urgently needed.

00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:57.123
JMR: "The world is thinking
wrong about race.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:02.577
They killed Sam Hose
for who they thought he was.

00:07:03.966 --> 00:07:05.910
And more men like him, every day,

00:07:05.934 --> 00:07:09.694
more Negro men, like him, taken apart.

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:13.856
And after that -- that red ray ...

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.647
we can never be the same.

00:07:17.665 --> 00:07:21.204
(Singing) A red ray

00:07:21.228 --> 00:07:22.481
[A man hunt in Georgia]

00:07:22.505 --> 00:07:24.435
cut across my desk

00:07:24.459 --> 00:07:26.648
[Mob after Hose;
he will be lynched if caught]

00:07:26.672 --> 00:07:28.918
the very day

00:07:28.942 --> 00:07:32.224
Sam's hands were laid to rest.

00:07:33.974 --> 00:07:40.951
Can words alone withstand the laws unjust?

00:07:40.975 --> 00:07:42.263
[Escape seems impossible]

00:07:42.287 --> 00:07:47.715
Can words alone withstand the violence?

00:07:50.038 --> 00:07:55.109
Oh, no, oh.

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:56.368
[Burned alive]

00:07:56.392 --> 00:07:57.727
[Sam Hose is lynched]

00:07:57.751 --> 00:08:02.656
Oh, no, oh.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.613
[His body cut in many pieces]

00:08:05.426 --> 00:08:08.410
Oh, no, oh.

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:09.831
[Burned at the Stake]

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:12.085
[Ten Cents Slice Cooked Liver.]

00:08:12.109 --> 00:08:13.575
[Fight for souvenirs.]

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.046
(Both singing) Who has read the books?

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:29.006
Our numbers and statistics look small

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:34.124
against the page.

00:08:34.148 --> 00:08:37.577
The crisis has multiplied.

00:08:37.601 --> 00:08:40.307
Our people are lynched and died.

00:08:40.331 --> 00:08:42.688
Oh, Lord.

00:08:42.712 --> 00:08:48.509
Something must change.

00:08:50.762 --> 00:08:55.371
AYGTK: Something must change.

00:08:56.397 --> 00:08:59.633
"At Buffalo" reveals
how the United States today

00:08:59.657 --> 00:09:03.197
stands at similar crossroads
as 1901 America.

00:09:03.617 --> 00:09:07.172
Just as the name of Sam Hose
filled newspapers back then,

00:09:07.196 --> 00:09:09.834
today's media carries the names of:

00:09:09.858 --> 00:09:11.521
JMR: Oscar Grant.

00:09:12.101 --> 00:09:13.523
MJ: Jacqueline Culp.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:15.377
Pianist: Trayvon Martin.

00:09:16.021 --> 00:09:17.355
AYGTK: Sandra Bland.

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:19.831
And too many others.

00:09:20.834 --> 00:09:24.849
The 1901 fair's legacies persist

00:09:24.873 --> 00:09:28.103
in more ways than we can imagine.

00:09:30.315 --> 00:09:31.498
MJ: Mary Talbert

00:09:31.522 --> 00:09:33.623
and the National Association
of Colored Women

00:09:33.647 --> 00:09:36.522
started movements against lynching

00:09:36.546 --> 00:09:38.537
and the myth of black criminality

00:09:38.561 --> 00:09:42.101
just as black women today
started Black Lives Matter.

00:09:42.601 --> 00:09:45.481
JMR: And some of the same
people who fought for

00:09:45.505 --> 00:09:47.357
and created the Negro Exhibit,

00:09:47.381 --> 00:09:49.292
including Du Bois,

00:09:49.316 --> 00:09:51.920
came to Buffalo,
four years after the fair,

00:09:51.944 --> 00:09:54.135
to start the Niagara Movement,

00:09:54.159 --> 00:09:57.506
which set the groundwork
for the creation of the NAACP.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.212
AYGTK: It's not just black folks

00:09:59.236 --> 00:10:02.030
who had a peculiar experience
at the 1901 fair.

00:10:02.403 --> 00:10:05.310
An official handbook informed fair-goers:

00:10:05.334 --> 00:10:07.349
MJ: "Please remember:"

00:10:07.373 --> 00:10:09.348
JMR: "... once you get inside the gate,"

00:10:09.372 --> 00:10:12.166
AYGTK: "... you are a part of the show."

00:10:13.071 --> 00:10:15.141
Performing the archive in "At Buffalo"

00:10:15.165 --> 00:10:17.768
allows audiences to ask themselves,

00:10:17.792 --> 00:10:20.307
"Are we still inside the gates,

00:10:20.331 --> 00:10:24.929
and are we all still part of the show?"

00:10:29.072 --> 00:10:32.485
(Music ends)

00:10:32.509 --> 00:10:39.396
(Applause and cheers)

